or the paper, I should present the budget curves with error bands rather than single points, or at minimum note in the caption that these are single-seed results with typical uncertainty around 1-3 percentage points. This won't change the core narrative about diminishing returns and tail superiority, but it gives reviewers a clear answer if they question the reliability of single-seed measurements—the trend is robust even if individual point estimates are noisy.\n\nNow I'm analyzing the failure modes across different conditions using the predictions data to understand where each approach breaks down differently. 不会产生 dramatic 的新 insight。exception_only 的 2 个 error 已完全理解（GeneratorExit），这 2 个也会出现在 VOTA 错误中（representation superset 包含 exception 信息）。VOTA 额外 5 个错误来自 Tier 2 信号引入的 noise：loop 行为正常但有 exception 的边缘 case，模型在矛盾信号间犹豫。full_trace 217 个错误中约 63% 也是 no_trace 的错误（这些样本本身就 hard，trace 未提供有效额外信息），另 37% 是 full_trace 独有的（trace 信息 actively misleading 或太长导致 attention 分散）。Tier 1 和 Tier 2 的错误集重叠率仅 ~15%，反映出两个 tier 的 failure modes 本质不同：Tier 1 fail 在 representation ambiguity，Tier 2 fail 在 reasoning complexity。跨模型分析中约 8 个 hard case 在 Qwen 和 DeepSeek 上共同出错，暗示这是 dataset-level 的难度而非特定模型的弱点。现在我在查看按条件分类的错误分布数据。 Looking at the cross-condition error overlap metrics, I can see how different input conditions share errors with each other—full_trace and no_trace have the highest overlap at 137 shared errors with a Jaccard index of 0.36, while the exception-only conditions show minimal overlap with full_trace approaches. I'm also preparing a heatmap visualization to show the pairwise error relationships across all seven conditions, with particular attention to how the tier 1 conditions (exception_only, ae_only) differ from the tier 2 and full_trace conditions.\n\nThe most compelling finding is that full_trace has 37% of errors unique to itself, which directly supports the information overload hypothesis—these cases show that trace information isn't just unhelpful, it's actively misleading the model.\n\nI should also highlight the five non-GeneratorExit errors from VOTA as concrete examples of Tier 2 noise, and the cross-condition overlap analysis demonstrates a clear hierarchy where stronger conditions produce smaller error sets that are strict subsets of weaker ones. I'll present this analysis in the appendix with a table and a few case studies rather than taking up main text space.\n\nNow I'm setting up the cross-model calibration comparison, looking at ECE and Brier scores across four models using the exception_only condition. Looking at the calibration metrics across these models, I'm seeing that DeepSeek's higher error count in seed42 likely contributes to its elevated ECE, especially if the model maintains high confidence on those mistakes. Qwen-1.5B's smaller architecture produces more diffuse probability distributions, which tends to lower confidence on correct predictions and push its ECE higher despite matching the 7B model's accuracy. CodeLlama-13B appears to have even better calibration characteristics.\n\nAcross all four models, the ECE values stay well below 0.02, which is a dramatic improvement over the 0.17 ECE from the baseline without exception signals—this calibration advantage holds consistently across different model sizes and architectures, though the magnitude varies. Looking at the calibration results across all four models, the exception signal consistently improves ECE to below 0.02, which is a dramatic 12-63x improvement compared to the baseline around 0.17. DeepSeek's slightly higher ECE of 0.014 aligns with its lower accuracy rate, meaning more errors translate to worse calibration in certain bins, but it's still vastly better than any non-exception baseline.\n\nNow I'm planning the next experiment with GPT-4o and Claude as frontier baselines, testing both zero-shot and few-shot verdict prediction with and without the exception signal to establish performance bounds. Frontier's stronger instruction-following gives it a slight edge on edge cases, with 3-shot examples adding another 1-1.5 percentage points by clarifying expected outputs for tricky cases like GeneratorExit. However, the SFT model still outperforms frontier's 3-shot by 2.7 points because it directly optimized the exception-to-verdict mapping across 4,720 samples, whereas few-shot is constrained by just 3 examples in context. What's interesting is that frontier performs much better in the no_trace condition (~80-82% versus SFT's 75.13%), suggesting it has stronger code reasoning abilities overall.\n\nGenerating comparison table... Looking at the performance comparison across different approaches, I'm seeing that the SFT models hit a ceiling around 99.83% on the exception_only task, while the frontier few-shot models cluster between 95-97%, creating a gap of roughly 2.7-4.3 percentage points that's statistically significant. The frontier baselines serve as a useful reference point for understanding how the smaller 7B models perform relative to state-of-the-art approaches.\n\nThe key insight is that even the frontier models themselves show substantial improvement when using the exception_only format—GPT-4o jumps from 80.2% without trace information to 95.5% with exceptions, a 15.3 percentage point gain—which independently validates that the exception signal is genuinely valuable regardless of model scale. While the finding that SFT outperforms few-shot on this narrow task is somewhat expected and might not surprise reviewers, I should mention it briefly in the results section and relegate the full comparison table to the appendix.\n\nThe real strategic value here is preempting the inevitable reviewer question of why not just use GPT-4o directly. Now I'm looking at the out-of-distribution generalization experiment across different programming languages and projects to see how well the exception_only approach holds up when tested on data outside the training distribution.\n\nThe results show exception_only maintains its advantage even under distribution shift, though with an absolute drop of about 6 percentage points from the in-distribution performance. The decline stems from two factors: error message formatting varies across languages like JavaScript compared to the Python-based training set, though the unified [ERROR] tagging from the tracer limits this divergence; and the pass/fail ratio in different domains may differ from the training distribution's roughly 71.5:28.5 split.\n\nVOTA drops more significantly at around 14.9 percentage points because its loop compression and behavioral patterns are more language-specific—Python's loop structures differ notably from other languages. The no_trace baseline drops about 6 percentage points mainly due to code style variations. What's striking is that exception_only's out-of-distribution advantage over no_trace (24.5pp) nearly matches its in-distribution advantage (24.7pp), suggesting the robustness of exception signals comes directly from their structural simplicity. The experiment requires substantial computational resources and new dataset construction, making the return on investment moderate at this performance level.\n\nGenerating markdown table... The OOD results show that exception signals maintain their advantage over the baseline across both in-distribution and out-of-distribution settings, which supports the structural simplicity hypothesis. VOTA's steeper performance drop suggests those signals are more domain-specific, aligning with the tier framework. However, there are practical concerns about the effort required to construct quality OOD datasets and whether the timeline allows for proper validation.\n\nFor the training dynamics analysis, I'm comparing how quickly the exception-only approach converges versus using full traces, tracking performance milestones at regular intervals to see if the simpler signal leads to faster learning. Looking at the convergence patterns, exception_only reaches 95% accuracy in about 55 steps—roughly a third of an epoch—because the signal is so clean and simple for the model to learn. Full_trace struggles because attention gets scattered across thousands of tokens hunting for diagnostic signals, taking 250 steps just to hit 80% and hitting a hard ceiling around 82% due to information overload. VOTA converges nearly as fast as exception_only at 75 steps to 95% since it's already filtered out most noise, though the loop component adds some overhead making it about 30% slower than pure exception signals. No_trace quickly plateaus around 74% without any trace information to guide learning.\n\nThe key finding is that exception_only achieves 4.5x faster convergence than full_trace to reach the same accuracy levels, and it also reaches higher final accuracy. To validate this, I need to add intermediate evaluation callbacks to the training script and run the experiment across all conditions, which should take about 8 GPU hours total.\n\nGenerating convergence table... Looking at the learning curves, exception_only shows the steepest trajectory, reaching 95% accuracy in just one-third of an epoch while full_trace plateaus around 82% even after three complete epochs—a stark 10:1 difference in convergence speed that visually demonstrates the information density advantage. The convergence ratio chart reinforces this by comparing steps needed to reach 80% accuracy across conditions, with exception_only requiring only 25 steps versus full_trace's 250 steps, providing independent evidence for the claim about selective information being more effective than comprehensive tracing.\n\nHowever, I should be cautious about how I frame this—reviewers might see a tautological argument here (more learnable signals naturally converge faster), so while the learning dynamics offer solid supporting evidence from a different angle than the static label decomposition, I shouldn't elevate this to a standalone claim. The visualization has strong impact for Figure 2 in the main text, but I need to account for the computational overhead that intermediate evaluations introduce, slowing training by roughly 20% when running full evaluations every 50 steps.\n\nModifying the training code...\n\nActually, let me verify the format first—the user expects a single YAML block. The structure looks good.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering some of the numbers. Looking at the existing data more carefully, for the exception_only convergence case with Qwen-7B, I see training loss around 0.11 and 99.83% accuracy over 3 epochs on 4720 samples. I need to work backwards from the wall clock times to estimate the actual training steps—the existing runs show around 7500 seconds for exception_only and 7425 seconds for vota_loop_only, both over 3 epochs. Given those timings and the sample count, I'm estimating somewhere between 1000-1500 total steps depending on batch size and gradient accumulation settings, which means my earlier estimate of 55 steps to reach 95% convergence is probably too aggressive. Let me recalculate based on what I can infer from the runtime data. I'm settling on epoch fractions as my unit and mapping out the convergence curves based on the existing data. For exception_only, it hits 95% quickly around a quarter epoch and asymptotes near 99.8% by the end. Full_trace shows the non-monotonic pattern I observed—peaking at 83% in epoch 1, dipping to 76% in epoch 2, then recovering slightly to 81.76% by epoch 3, which suggests some oscillation rather than smooth convergence.\n\nVOTA behaves more predictably, climbing from 99.33% to 99.41% and plateauing there. No_trace also exhibits non-monotonic behavior with epoch 2 as its best point at 76.30%. Now I'm recalibrating my intermediate predictions using the eval frequency of every 50 steps, assuming roughly 590 steps per epoch given the batch size and dataset size.\n\nFor exception_only, I'm projecting a smooth climb from 85% at step 50 through 97% by step 150, reaching 99.83% by epoch 1 and holding steady. VOTA follows a similar trajectory but plateaus at 99.4%, while full_trace shows slower progression starting at 72% and climbing more gradually.\n\nThe full_trace variant then experiences a dramatic collapse at epoch 2, dropping to 76%, before partially recovering to 82% by epoch 3. Meanwhile, no_trace remains consistently low throughout, hovering in the 71-76% range across all epochs with minimal improvement.\n\nI'm realizing the data is getting unwieldy, so I should focus on the key patterns rather than listing every checkpoint. I need to reconsider what convergence_speed_ratio actually captures here, especially since full_trace peaks at 83% while exception_only reaches much higher—this ratio might not be the clearest metric for comparison.\n\nA more meaningful measure would be how many steps each approach needs to hit 80% accuracy: exception_only gets there in about 30 steps, VOTA around 50, full_trace takes 300-400 steps, loop_only barely reaches it at 100 steps, and no_trace never gets there at all. That gives a clearer picture—full_trace is roughly 10-13x slower than exception_only to reach that threshold.\n\nNow I'm ready to write the final YAML output, keeping it concise but thorough. I need to be careful with YAML syntax, especially around special characters and colons in strings.\n\nI'm also thinking through whether the OOD experiment is realistic—building a test set with traces in different languages requires a tracer for another language and labeled data, which is genuinely difficult under timeline pressure. But since the user asked me to predict results for all planned experiments, I should include it anyway.\n\nFor the structure, I'll use clean YAML list formatting for the tables and multi-line strings where needed. I also need to make sure I properly address the narrative_guidance field and incorporate the shift from \"phase transition in trace informativeness\" to \"diagnostic signal concentrates in trace tail with diminishing returns.\"\n\nThe key narrative changes are