il-1024 — the v1 run failed entirely while v2 achieved 87.6% accuracy.\n\nLooking at the tail-2048 results for CodeLlama, both v2 and v3 show severe degradation (29.58% and 41.8% respectively), indicating model collapse at that budget level. This means the budget curve for CodeLlama isn't monotonic at the high end, which I'll need to account for when determining which models to include in the consistent budget curve for the upcoming plans.\n\nChecking the other models' tail data, DeepSeek shows a slight dip from tail-1024 (96.47%) to tail-2048 (94.96%) — only about 1.5 percentage points, which is minor compared to CodeLlama's collapse. Qwen-1.5B's tail progression appears to be continuing upward, though the data cuts off mid-entry. Now I'm clarifying what the sharpness index actually measures—it captures whether the performance jump happens sharply between 256→384 tokens (index near 1), gradually across the range (index around 0.5), or mostly between 384→512 (index near 0). The gradual range of 0.35 to 0.65 aligns with my tier definition, so I'm confident in this framework. Looking at the few-shot results for Qwen-7B, there's a modest improvement from zero-shot to few-shot performance on the exception-only task, which I should factor into the final output.\n\nFor frontier models, I'd expect a different pattern—they should have higher baselines due to better instruction following, but smaller gains from few-shot since they're already strong at zero-shot. So GPT-4o and Claude should land around 95-97% at zero-shot with only 1-1.5 percentage points of improvement from few-shot. But the zero-shot numbers I'm seeing across the smaller models are interesting: Qwen-1.5B surprisingly hits 93.45%, while DeepSeek-7B and Qwen-7B are at 90.59% and 89.08% respectively.\n\nThe 1.5B model outperforming the 7B is unexpected—likely a difference in instruction tuning or how well the specific prompt aligns with that model's training. Looking at this more carefully, the zero-shot task is essentially asking models to learn a simple heuristic from the exception trace: does it contain [ERROR]? If yes, predict fail; otherwise, pass. That baseline heuristic gets 93.45%, which matches the 1.5B performance exactly. So frontier models with superior instruction following should reliably execute this heuristic at 95-97% zero-shot, with the remaining errors coming from edge cases or misinterpretations.\n\nAdding three-shot examples should help models handle those edge cases better, pushing performance up another 1-2 percentage points. The SFT approach reaches 99.83% because it has thousands of training examples that let it learn the nuances beyond the simple rule.\n\nNow I'm thinking about how to structure the output. The user asked specifically about each planned experiment in the plan, so I should focus on plans 016-022 rather than treating the what_if experiments separately. Since there's overlap between them, I'll integrate the what_if results into the relevant plan entries.\n\nGenerating the YAML output with all seven planned experiments...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":41629,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":41629},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ca021833-68ad-48d2-8c47-9a2b5434c32f","uuid":"586ab151-0d22-492d-903f-3e2634a639bc"}
